{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For managing acid reflux, focus on foods that are naturally low in fat and acid, and high in fiber or water content. Here are the top choices, distilled from expert sources:\n\n**Best Foods for Acid Reflux Relief**\n\n- **Green & Leafy Vegetables:** Broccoli, spinach, kale, asparagus, cucumbers, and celery are all low in fat and acid, helping reduce stomach acid and soothe symptoms.\n- **Oatmeal & Whole Grains:** Oatmeal is especially effective due to its fiber content, which absorbs stomach acid. Wholegrain rice and breads are also good options.\n- **Bananas & Melons:** Both are low-acid, alkaline fruits that can coat and protect the esophagus, reducing discomfort.\n- **Ginger:** Naturally anti-inflammatory, ginger (fresh or as tea) calms the digestive tract and helps prevent reflux.\n- **Yogurt (Low-Fat):** Provides a soothing effect and supports gut health. Choose non-fat or low-fat varieties.\n- **Lean Proteins:** Skinless chicken, turkey, and fish (especially oily fish like salmon and trout) are less likely to trigger reflux than fatty meats.\n- **Nuts & Healthy Fats:** Almonds, walnuts, flaxseed, and avocados offer healthy fats that are less likely to aggravate symptoms.\n- **High-Water Foods:** Watermelon, celery, and broths help dilute stomach acid.\n\n**Foods to Avoid:** Citrus fruits, high-fat foods, and anything spicy or heavily processed.\n\nWould you like meal ideas using these ingredients, or tips on how to incorporate them into your daily routine?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0044-72-05971:1": -0.014405302703380585, "clueweb22-en0042-37-07571:2": -0.015688449144363403, "clueweb22-en0015-18-13111:5": -0.017260806635022163, "clueweb22-en0034-72-18979:0": -0.019336648285388947, "clueweb22-en0009-09-00824:2": -0.02040618658065796}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I want to change my lifestyle.", "I like a variety 