{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Apple cider vinegar (ACV) is indeed acidic, but its use for acid reflux is controversial and depends on the type of reflux you have.\n\n**Key Points:**\n\n- **Traditional Acid Reflux (GERD):**\n  - Some sources suggest that diluted ACV (1–2 tablespoons in water before meals) may help if your reflux is due to low stomach acid, as it can aid digestion and restore stomach pH balance.\n  - Only raw, organic, unfiltered ACV is recommended if you choose to try this.\n  - There is limited scientific evidence supporting its effectiveness, and more research is needed.\n\n- **Silent Reflux (LPR) or Severe Symptoms:**\n  - ACV is not recommended. Its acidity can worsen symptoms by activating pepsin outside the stomach, increasing irritation in the throat and airways.\n\n- **Safety:**\n  - Never drink ACV undiluted—it can damage tooth enamel and irritate your throat.\n  - If you notice worsening symptoms after trying ACV, stop immediately.\n\n**Personalized Recommendation:**\nGiven your concern about acidity, ACV may not be the best first-line remedy, especially if your symptoms are severe or include throat irritation. Safer, evidence-backed options include non-citrus fruits, vegetables, lean proteins, and lifestyle changes (eating earlier, weight management).\n\nWould you like more natural remedies with a gentler profile, or guidance on when to see a doctor for acid reflux?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": -0.013133566826581955, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:1": -0.025086309760808945, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:3": -0.02727603353559971, "clueweb22-en0027-96-01816:1": -0.03452036529779434, "clueweb22-en0019-74-15565:5": -0.04044709354639053}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I want to change my lifestyle.", "I like a variety of fruits.", "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.", "I s