{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Acid reflux happens when stomach acid flows back into the esophagus, often due to a weakened or relaxed lower esophageal sphincter (LES). The underlying reasons can include:\n\n- **Low Stomach Acid:** Surprisingly, insufficient stomach acid is a common cause. When acid levels are low, the LES may relax more often, allowing acid to escape upward. This can also impair digestion and nutrient absorption. (Multiple sources)\n- **Aging:** Stomach acid production naturally decreases with age, increasing reflux risk.\n- **Diet and Lifestyle:** Stress, poor diet (especially high in refined sugars), and certain medications can reduce stomach acid or weaken the LES.\n- **Overuse of Antacids:** Frequent use of antacids or acid blockers can further lower stomach acid, worsening the problem.\n- **Other Factors:** Overgrowth of yeast (candida), vitamin/mineral deficiencies, and drinking too much water with meals can also contribute.\n\n**Why Apple Cider Vinegar Sometimes Helps:**\n- For those with low stomach acid, adding a small amount of apple cider vinegar before meals can help restore acidity, prompting the LES to close properly and improving digestion. However, if symptoms worsen, discontinue use.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**  \nAcid reflux is often a sign of an imbalance in your digestive system, frequently linked to low stomach acid rather than too much. Addressing diet, stress, and meal timing can help restore balance.\n\nWould you like tips on how to naturally support healthy stomach acid levels or more on lifestyle changes to prevent reflux?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-27-00769:8": -0.06808631122112274, "clueweb22-en0025-26-18393:8": -0.06862538307905197, "clueweb22-en0046-28-07406:3": -0.07818939536809921, "clueweb22-en0030-73-19245:1": -0.08264914900064468, "clueweb22-en0022-52-08268:12": -0.08958599716424942}, "ptkb_p