{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You asked if your approaching deadline (stress) could be related to your acid reflux, and about over-the-counter (OTC) medication options.\n\n**Stress & Acid Reflux**\n- Stress is a well-known trigger for acid reflux. It can increase stomach acid production and heighten your sensitivity to reflux symptoms.\n- Stress may also lead to behaviors that worsen reflux, such as unhealthy eating, skipping exercise, or smoking.\n\n**OTC Medications for Acid Reflux**\n- **Antacids** (e.g., Tums, Maalox, Mylanta, Rolaids): Quickly neutralize stomach acid. Best for occasional, mild symptoms. Overuse can cause diarrhea or constipation.\n- **H2 Blockers** (e.g., Pepcid [famotidine], Tagamet [cimetidine]): Reduce acid production. Provide longer-lasting relief than antacids.\n- **Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs)** (e.g., Prilosec [omeprazole], Nexium [esomeprazole], Prevacid [lansoprazole]): Most effective for frequent symptoms. Heal esophageal lining over time.\n- **Foaming agents** (e.g., Gaviscon): Coat the stomach to prevent reflux.\n\n**Personalized Guidance**\n- Since your reflux may be stress-related, combining stress management (exercise, relaxation techniques) with medication could be most effective.\n- If you try OTC meds, start with antacids for quick relief. If symptoms persist, consider H2 blockers or PPIs, but consult your doctor if you need them regularly.\n\nWould you like tips on stress management techniques that specifically help with reflux, or guidance on choosing between these medication types?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-13-07696:11": -0.04621506482362747, "clueweb22-en0038-68-03750:11": -0.10733529180288315, "clueweb22-en0014-47-04474:14": -0.12189138680696487, "clueweb22-en0002-04-09789:3": -0.15360558032989502, "clueweb22-en0003-24-03853:7": -0.18814902007579803}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I want to change m