{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s a concise, side-by-side comparison of the two main over-the-counter (OTC) medication classes for acid reflux—H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs)—tailored to your need for fast, effective relief under deadline stress:\n\n**Effectiveness**\n- **PPIs (e.g., omeprazole, esomeprazole, lansoprazole, pantoprazole):**\n  - Block acid production more powerfully and for a longer duration.\n  - Superior for moderate to severe or persistent symptoms.\n  - Promote healing of esophageal inflammation.\n- **H2 Blockers (e.g., famotidine, cimetidine, nizatidine):**\n  - Reduce acid by blocking histamine’s effect on stomach cells.\n  - Work faster for short-term, mild symptoms.\n  - Less effective than PPIs for severe or chronic reflux.\n\n**How to Take**\n- **PPIs:** Best taken 30–60 minutes before meals, usually once daily.\n- **H2 Blockers:** Can be taken 30 minutes before meals or at bedtime for nighttime symptoms.\n\n**Side Effects**\n- Both are generally well-tolerated. Possible side effects include headache, abdominal pain, diarrhea, and nausea.\n\n**Special Notes**\n- Ranitidine (an H2 blocker) was removed from the market due to safety concerns.\n- Do not combine a PPI and H2 blocker unless directed by a doctor.\n\n**Personalized Recommendation**\n- If your symptoms are frequent or severe (especially under stress), a PPI is likely more effective.\n- For occasional, mild symptoms, an H2 blocker may suffice.\n\nWould you like guidance on how to start one of these medications, or tips for managing reflux during stressful periods?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-30-11232:7": -0.027256080880761147, "clueweb22-en0027-47-12028:3": -0.029317433014512062, "clueweb22-en0023-88-10344:1": -0.03261915594339371, "clueweb22-en0004-00-09704:7": -0.03285900875926018, "clueweb22-en0008-05-05529:8": -0.03390980884432793}, "