{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To help you, as a software engineer who eats out often and tends to have late dinners, here are practical, sustainable strategies to make your lifestyle healthier and more active—without unrealistic overhauls:\n\n**1. Smarter Eating Out**\n- **Pack or Prep When Possible:** Bringing your own food (even just snacks) gives you control over nutrition—think almonds, turkey slices, fruit, or low-fat dairy. If you must eat out, opt for grilled proteins, salads, or vegetable-based dishes.\n- **Portion Control:** Use smaller plates or consciously halve restaurant portions. Take leftovers home to avoid overeating.\n- **Choose Lighter Dinners:** Especially if eating late, go for lighter options like soups, salads, grilled fish/chicken, or stir-fried veggies. Avoid heavy, rich meals at night.\n\n**2. Manage Late-Night Eating**\n- **Aim for Earlier Dinners:** If possible, try to eat by 8–9 PM. If your schedule doesn’t allow, ensure there’s at least a 2-hour gap between dinner and sleep.\n- **Meal Prep for Convenience:** Prepare simple, healthy meals in advance (e.g., soups, stir-fries) and refrigerate them for quick access after work.\n\n**3. Build Healthy Habits Around Your Routine**\n- **Stick to a Meal Schedule:** Regular meal times help prevent overeating at night.\n- **Snack Wisely:** Choose healthy snacks like fruits, boiled eggs, or roasted chickpeas instead of processed options.\n- **Limit Distractions:** Avoid screens during meals to prevent mindless overeating.\n\n**4. Small Activity Boosts**\n- Walk or bike for short commutes.\n- Take short movement breaks during coding sessions.\n\nWould you like quick meal prep ideas or tips for fitting activity into a busy workday?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0002-99-03435:9": -0.09166018664836884, "clueweb22-en0002-99-03435:8": -0.12231088429689407, "clueweb22-en0029-04-16046:1