{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re looking for ways to make coffee taste better because you dislike the core flavor, not just the bitterness or additives.\n\n**Top Strategies to Make Coffee Taste Less Like Coffee:**\n\n- **Add Flavors:**  \n  - Vanilla extract or syrup (add a few drops to the grounds before brewing for a smoother, sweeter cup).\n  - Cinnamon, cardamom, cocoa powder, or even a hint of peppermint oil can mask the coffee taste and add complexity.\n  - Coconut or vanilla syrups are especially effective for sweetness and balance.\n\n- **Creaminess:**  \n  - Use milk, half-and-half, or plant-based alternatives (almond, oat, coconut milk) to mellow the flavor.\n  - Heavy cream is particularly good at reducing bitterness and acidity, making coffee taste richer and less “coffee-like.”\n\n- **Cold Brew:**  \n  - Try making cold brew instead of hot coffee. This method produces a milder, less acidic, and slightly sweeter drink that many people who dislike regular coffee enjoy.\n\n- **Dilute:**  \n  - Brew a weaker cup or add extra hot water (Americano style) to reduce intensity.\n\n**Personalized Recommendation:**  \nSince your issue is with the taste of coffee itself, start by experimenting with cold brew and adding vanilla or coconut flavoring. Combine with a creamy base (dairy or non-dairy) for the smoothest, least “coffee-tasting” result.\n\nWould you like a simple recipe for cold brew or tips on specific flavor combinations to try?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0029-28-15775:7": -0.015376708470284939, "clueweb22-en0022-12-19245:1": -0.020717063918709755, "clueweb22-en0029-28-15775:6": -0.020950332283973694, "clueweb22-en0031-22-10254:13": -0.02200496941804886, "clueweb22-en0026-83-04609:17": -0.02377571165561676}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I want to lose weight.", "I don't eat sugar.", "I occasionally experience mild acid reflux and pref