{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s a concise guide to coffee types, focused on helping you avoid sour flavors and find a taste you’ll enjoy:\n\n**Roast Levels & Taste**\n- **Light Roast:** Highest acidity, often tastes sour or fruity. Not recommended if you dislike sourness.\n- **Medium Roast:** Balanced flavor, slight acidity, generally smoother and less sour than light roasts. Good starting point if you want a milder, well-rounded cup.\n- **Dark Roast:** Little to no acidity, more bitter and smoky with caramel notes. Least likely to taste sour—best match for your preference.\n\n**Bean Origin & Type**\n- **Arabica Beans:** Can taste fruity and sharp, especially in lighter roasts—sometimes perceived as sour.\n- **Blends (Arabica + Robusta):** Often smoother and creamier, with chocolatey notes and less sharpness.\n\n**Other Factors**\n- **Stale Beans:** Old beans taste off and sour. Always use fresh beans.\n- **Water & Grind:** Use filtered water and ensure your grind size matches your brewing method to avoid under-extraction (which causes sourness).\n\n**Recommendation for You**\n- Choose a **dark roast** or a **medium-dark blend** (especially those with chocolate or caramel notes).\n- Look for blends that include Robusta for a smoother, less acidic profile.\n- Avoid light roasts and single-origin Arabicas if you’re sensitive to sourness.\n\nWould you like specific brand or blend recommendations, or tips on brewing methods to further reduce sourness?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0011-87-01475:7": -0.027187984436750412, "clueweb22-en0011-87-01475:6": -0.028244758024811745, "clueweb22-en0003-82-03894:2": -0.052639517933130264, "clueweb22-en0022-18-11457:2": -0.05420106649398804, "clueweb22-en0011-03-12177:6": -0.061614494770765305}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I want to lose weight.", "I don't eat sugar.", "I start my day by drinking coffee.", "I occ