{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Roasting is the single most important factor shaping the final taste, aroma, and body of your coffee—especially for unique beans like Liberica and Excelsa.\n\n**How Roasting Impacts Flavor:**\n- **Flavor Development:** Roasting transforms green coffee’s raw, grassy flavors into the complex aromas and tastes you expect. This happens through chemical reactions (Maillard, caramelization) that create hundreds of new flavor compounds.\n- **Light to Medium Roasts:** For Excelsa, medium-light roasts highlight berry-like, fruity, and even popcorn notes. These roasts preserve acidity and complexity.\n- **Darker Roasts:** Both Liberica and Excelsa develop fuller body and deeper flavors as roast darkens. Excelsa shifts toward chocolate and cream notes; Liberica’s signature smoky, woody, and floral qualities become more pronounced.\n- **Body and Acidity:** Lighter roasts tend to be brighter and more acidic, while darker roasts are richer, heavier, and less acidic—often preferred if you dislike sourness.\n\n**Special Considerations for Liberica & Excelsa:**\n- **Liberica:** Its bold, woody character stands up well to medium-dark roasts, which smooth out sharpness and enhance body.\n- **Excelsa:** Needs careful roasting (often hotter or longer) due to its dense structure. Roasting too light can leave it underdeveloped; too dark can mask its unique fruitiness.\n\n**Personalized Tip:** Since you dislike sourness and prefer a rich, smooth cup, choose a medium-dark roast of Liberica. This will minimize acidity and highlight the bean’s distinctive, full-bodied flavors.\n\nWould you like advice on brewing methods to further enhance these flavors?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-97-11464:11": -0.009612937457859516, "clueweb22-en0004-97-11464:12": -0.012570788152515888, "clueweb22-en0016-45-10462:1": -0.02420877292752266, "clueweb22