{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Absolutely—there are many wines known for their “sour” or tart qualities, which typically means they have higher acidity. Here’s how to identify and select them:\n\n**What Makes a Wine Taste Sour?**\n- **High acidity** is the main driver of sourness in wine. Wines from cooler climates tend to be more acidic and thus taste more tart or crisp.\n\n**Classic “Sour” Wine Styles:**\n- **White Wines:**  \n  - **Sauvignon Blanc:** Especially from cool regions (e.g., Loire Valley, New Zealand). Expect flavors like green apple, lime, grapefruit, and a refreshing, mouth-puckering brightness.\n  - **Riesling:** German and Alsatian Rieslings are famously high in acidity, delivering sharp, zesty flavors (think green apple, lemon, sometimes even a hint of petrol).\n  - **Pinot Gris/Pinot Grigio:** From cooler climates, these can be crisp and tart.\n- **Red Wines:**  \n  - **Pinot Noir:** Particularly from Burgundy or other cool regions, offers tart cherry, cranberry, and earthy notes.\n  - **Barbera and Sangiovese:** Italian reds known for their lively acidity and sour cherry flavors.\n\n**How to Find Them:**\n- Look for wines from **cooler regions** (Germany, Northern France, New Zealand, Northern Italy).\n- Seek out descriptors like “crisp,” “zesty,” “tart,” or “bright” on labels or tasting notes.\n\n**Personalized Tip:**  \nSince you previously preferred less sour coffee, these wines will be the opposite—great if you want to explore the “sour” side of wine.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific bottles or regions to try first?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0025-15-15529:3": -0.05557120218873024, "clueweb22-en0028-55-10550:12": -0.05610669404268265, "clueweb22-en0006-72-02275:9": -0.08070188015699387, "clueweb22-en0040-18-06522:7": -0.08746861666440964, "clueweb22-en0006-72-02275:13": -0.09746318310499191}, "ptkb_prove