{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "2-2_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "If you want a wine that truly tastes like freshly cut grass, Sauvignon Blanc is your best match.\n\n**Why Sauvignon Blanc?**\n- **Signature Flavor:** Sauvignon Blanc is renowned for its distinct aroma and flavor of fresh-cut grass—this is its hallmark, especially from New Zealand and France.\n- **Other Notes:** Alongside grassy notes, expect hints of lime, green apple, gooseberry, and sometimes green bell pepper or lemongrass.\n- **Style:** Typically dry, crisp, and refreshing, with lively acidity that enhances the green, herbaceous character.\n- **Best Regions:** New Zealand (especially Marlborough) produces some of the most pronounced grassy Sauvignon Blancs. French versions (Loire Valley, Sancerre) are also excellent, often with a slightly more mineral edge.\n\n**Other Options:**\n- **Grüner Veltliner (Austria):** Offers green apple and citrus, sometimes with a subtle grassy or white pepper note, but less overtly grassy than Sauvignon Blanc.\n- **Pinot Grigio:** Can be crisp and refreshing, but the grassy note is much less pronounced.\n\n**Personalized Justification:**  \nYou specifically asked for the \"freshly cut grass\" flavor—Sauvignon Blanc is the textbook example. New Zealand labels like Kim Crawford, Matua, or Oyster Bay are widely available and consistently deliver this profile.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific bottles or tips on food pairings to enhance the grassy notes?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0000-05-02127:6": -0.01323297992348671, "clueweb22-en0028-55-10550:13": -0.01682976633310318, "clueweb22-en0017-93-02056:3": -0.017027612775564194, "clueweb22-en0044-31-17864:17": -0.017213821411132812, "clueweb22-en0018-56-18534:1": -0.019806815311312675}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I occasionally experience mild acid reflux and prefer beverages that are gentle on the stomach.", "I like to try som