{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re an experienced outdoor climber with finger pain. Here’s what matters most for your situation:\n\n**Most Likely Cause**\n- Finger pain in regular climbers is often due to overuse injuries, especially to the flexor tendon sheath or pulley system (A2 pulley injuries are most common).\n- These injuries typically present as pain at the base of the finger, sometimes with swelling or a “pop” sensation. Severe cases may show bulging (“bowstringing”) when bending the finger.\n\n**Immediate Actions**\n- **Stop climbing on the injured finger**—continuing can worsen the injury and prolong recovery.\n- **RICE protocol**: Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation. Ice your finger several times a day.\n- Keep your hands clean and moisturized to help with skin sensitivity.\n\n**When to Seek Medical Attention**\n- If you notice significant swelling, bruising, or bulging at the base of the finger, or if you heard a “pop,” consult a sports medicine or orthopedic specialist. These can indicate a serious pulley rupture.\n\n**Prevention for Future Sessions**\n- Warm up fingers thoroughly before climbing.\n- Avoid full crimping; use open-hand or half-crimp grips when possible.\n- Gradually increase intensity on finger-intensive routes.\n- Use finger strengthening tools (bands, grippers) off the wall.\n\n**Tailored Advice**\nGiven your experience and outdoor focus, you’re likely pushing your fingers hard. Prioritize rest and recovery now to avoid long-term downtime.\n\nWould you like guidance on specific rehab exercises or tips for returning to climbing safely after recovery?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:2": -0.06093950942158699, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:1": -0.06474318355321884, "clueweb22-en0023-44-07422:0": -0.06803631037473679, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": -0.07192552089691162, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -0.0720