{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your regular outdoor climbing and recent finger pain, here’s what matters most:\n\n**Most Likely Causes (Given Your Profile):**\n- **Finger Pulley Injury:** The most common finger injury in climbers, especially after years of regular climbing. Typically affects the A2 or A4 pulley, often in the middle or ring finger. Signs include pain at the base of the finger, swelling, and pain when bending or straightening the finger. Sometimes a “pop” is felt at the time of injury.\n- **Overuse/Tendinopathy:** Repetitive strain from climbing small holds can cause tendon or ligament irritation, leading to persistent finger pain.\n- **Less Common but Serious (Especially in Younger Climbers):** Stress fractures at the finger joints, usually with point tenderness and swelling.\n\n**What to Do Now:**\n- **Stop Climbing on the Injured Finger:** Continuing to climb can turn a minor injury into a long-term problem.\n- **Monitor for Red Flags:** If you notice bowstringing (bulging at the base of the finger), severe swelling, or inability to move the finger, seek medical attention—these can indicate a serious tear.\n- **Initial Self-Care:** Rest, ice, and gentle movement (if not too painful) are recommended. Avoid aggressive stretching or loading.\n- **Consider Professional Assessment:** Especially if pain persists beyond a few days or worsens, consult a sports medicine doctor or a physiotherapist familiar with climbing injuries.\n\nWould you like guidance on specific rehab exercises, or help finding a climbing-savvy medical professional near you?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-77-07377:1": -0.04232652485370636, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -0.06957679241895676, "clueweb22-en0012-45-12823:1": -0.06996124237775803, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": -0.07430282235145569, "clueweb22-en0042-26-18398:10": -0.08643732964992523}, "