{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s a concise, climber-focused overview of finger anatomy and injury localization to help you identify your pain:\n\n**Finger Anatomy Relevant to Climbing Injuries**\n- **Bones & Joints:** Each finger has three bones (phalanges) and three joints.\n- **Tendons:** Flexor tendons run along the palm side, connecting forearm muscles to the finger bones. These allow you to bend your fingers.\n- **Pulleys:** Five annular pulleys (A1–A5) are bands of connective tissue that hold the flexor tendons close to the bone, especially during gripping. The A2 pulley (at the base of the finger) is most commonly injured in climbers.\n\n**Common Climbing-Related Finger Injuries**\n- **Pulley Injuries:** Often caused by crimping or dynamic moves. Symptoms include:\n  - Pain at the base of the finger (especially A2 pulley)\n  - Swelling, sometimes a “pop” at injury\n  - Pain or weakness when bending/straightening the finger\n  - In severe cases, visible bulging (“bowstringing”) when flexing the finger\n- **Flexor Tendon Injuries:** Less common but serious. Signs include:\n  - Inability to bend the finger\n  - Pain or tenderness along the palm side\n  - Numbness in the fingertip\n\n**How to Localize Your Pain**\n- **Pulley (A2):** Pain at the base of the finger, especially when crimping or resisting finger bending.\n- **Tendon:** Pain or tenderness along the length of the finger on the palm side, possibly with loss of movement.\n\n**Next Step:**  \nCan you describe if your pain is at the base of the finger, along the palm side, or if you notice swelling or bulging? This will help narrow down the likely cause and best next actions.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -0.024997951462864876, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -0.07252461463212967, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:0": -0.0858655497431755, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": -