{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s how finger pain presents in different areas relevant to climbing injuries, so you can better localize your pain:\n\n**Pulley Injuries (especially A2, A3, A4)**\n- **Location:** Pain is usually at the base of the finger (A2 is most common for climbers).\n- **Symptoms:** Pain when bending or straightening the finger, sometimes swelling. A loud “pop” at the time of injury is classic. In severe cases, you may see “bowstringing”—a visible bulge at the base of the finger when you try to bend it.\n- **Aggravating Movements:** Crimping or gripping small holds increases pain.\n- **Severity Clue:** Bowstringing or inability to bend the finger strongly suggests a serious pulley rupture—see a specialist if this occurs.\n\n**Tendon Injuries**\n- **Location:** Pain and tenderness along the palm side of the finger.\n- **Symptoms:** Pain when trying to bend the finger, sometimes inability to fully bend one or more finger joints. May also feel numbness at the fingertip.\n- **Aggravating Movements:** Any attempt to flex (bend) the finger, especially against resistance.\n- **Severity Clue:** If you cannot bend the finger at all, or if there’s a deep cut, seek medical attention urgently.\n\n**Joint Injuries (e.g., PIP joint)**\n- **Location:** Pain at the middle joint (dorsal or top side).\n- **Symptoms:** Point tenderness, swelling, sometimes after a sudden event or gradually with overuse.\n- **Aggravating Movements:** Pressing on the joint or bending it.\n\n**Next Step:**  \nCan you describe exactly where the pain is (base, middle, or tip of finger; palm side or top)? Does it hurt more when you bend, straighten, or press on a specific spot? This will help pinpoint the likely cause and best next actions.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": -0.02633388713002205, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": -0.02871955931186676, "