{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your description—pain in the front of your finger, especially when crimping, but no visible bowstringing—here’s what’s most relevant:\n\n**A2 Pulley Strain Is Still Likely**\n- Pain at the front of the finger during crimping is classic for an A2 pulley strain, even without visible bowstringing. Most A2 pulley injuries do not result in obvious bowstringing unless the rupture is complete, which is rare.\n- Pulley strains are much more likely with a full crimp grip, matching your climbing activity and symptoms.\n\n**Severity and Signs**\n- Mild to moderate A2 pulley strains often present as pain (sometimes swelling) at the base or front of the finger, especially when gripping or crimping.\n- Bowstringing (a visible bulge when flexing the finger) typically only appears with a complete rupture. Absence of this sign suggests a partial strain or minor tear, which is more common.\n\n**Other Causes Less Likely**\n- Collateral ligament injuries cause pain on the sides of the finger, not the front.\n- Tendon injuries usually involve difficulty bending the finger or pain extending toward the wrist, which you haven’t described.\n\n**What to Do Next**\n- Rest the finger and avoid crimping for now.\n- Consider taping the finger for support if you return to climbing.\n- Gradually reintroduce finger loading with gentle exercises once pain subsides.\n- If pain persists or worsens, consult a hand specialist.\n\nWould you like specific taping techniques or a step-by-step rehab plan tailored for climbers?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": -0.013342850841581821, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": -0.19052354991436005, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": -0.2602909505367279}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:16": -0.013342850841581821, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": -0.19052354991436005, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:9": -0.2602909505367279}}
{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your description—pain in the front of the finger when crimping, no visible bowstringing—this is most consistent with a mild to moderate A2 pulley strain, not a full rupture. Here’s what you should do next:\n\n**Immediate Steps**\n- **Stop climbing and avoid stressing the finger** (especially crimping) until pain subsides.\n- **Ice and NSAIDs** are only necessary if you have visible swelling.\n- **Gentle massage** (or using tools like acupressure rings) can help increase blood flow and support healing.\n\n**Rehabilitation**\n- **Gentle range-of-motion exercises:** Once acute pain decreases, bend and straighten the finger within a pain-free range (hold for 2–3 seconds, repeat 10x, several times daily).\n- **No immobilization is needed** for mild strains, but avoid pushing through pain.\n- **When pain and swelling are gone** (typically 1–4 weeks), begin light strengthening (e.g., squeezing putty or a stress ball).\n\n**Return to Climbing**\n- Only resume climbing when you have full, pain-free range of motion.\n- Start with easy routes, avoid crimping, and consider protective taping for the first few months.\n- Full return to normal climbing is usually possible in 6 weeks for mild injuries.\n\n**When to Seek Medical Attention**\n- If you develop visible bowstringing, severe swelling, or cannot bend the finger, see a hand specialist for imaging.\n\nWould you like specific taping instructions or guidance on safe exercises to start your rehab?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:3": -0.026342246681451797, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:4": -0.02692088857293129, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": -0.028050623834133148, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:11": -0.04057427495718002, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5": -0.04570021480321884}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Many years ago I had