{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "If you continue climbing without resting your injured finger—especially if it’s an A2 pulley strain—the main risks are:\n\n**1. Worsening the Injury**\n- Climbing on a strained or partially torn pulley can turn a minor injury into a full rupture, which is much more serious and may require months of recovery or even surgery.\n- Tendons and pulleys heal slowly; continued stress can delay or prevent proper healing, leading to chronic pain or weakness.\n\n**2. Prolonged Downtime**\n- Pushing through pain often results in a much longer total recovery period. What could heal in 4–6 weeks with rest might take several months if aggravated.\n\n**3. Compensatory Injuries**\n- You may unconsciously alter your grip or technique to avoid pain, increasing the risk of injuring other fingers, tendons, or even your shoulder and elbow.\n\n**4. Decreased Performance**\n- Persistent pain and incomplete healing can limit your climbing ability long-term.\n\n**What’s Recommended for Climbers Like You:**\n- Rest from climbing and avoid crimping or hangboarding until pain-free.\n- Gentle range-of-motion exercises are safe if they don’t cause pain.\n- Resume climbing only when you can use the finger without discomfort.\n\n**Bottom Line:** Not resting risks turning a manageable strain into a major, career-impacting injury. Prioritize healing now to protect your long-term climbing.\n\nWould you like guidance on a specific rehab protocol or tips for maintaining fitness while you rest your finger?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": -0.19614145159721375, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:5": -0.2713110148906708, "clueweb22-en0023-98-00232:9": -0.28688785433769226, "clueweb22-en0005-83-07260:6": -0.2948188781738281, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:1": -0.30146029591560364}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Ma