{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When returning to outdoor climbing after an A2 pulley injury, your main goals are to protect the healing tissue, avoid reinjury, and gradually rebuild strength. Here’s a focused, actionable plan tailored to your situation:\n\n**1. Start Easy and Controlled**\n- Begin with easy routes featuring large holds and good footholds. Avoid overhangs, small crimps, and pockets for several months, as these put the most stress on the A2 pulley.\n- Keep sessions short and stop immediately if you feel pain.\n\n**2. Use Taping for Support**\n- Apply H-taping to the injured finger when you climb. This provides extra support and serves as a reminder to protect the area. Taping is not a substitute for healing but helps reduce load during the return phase.\n\n**3. Gradual Progression**\n- Only increase difficulty if you can climb pain-free on easy routes.\n- Avoid dynamic moves and fully closed crimp grips, which dramatically increase pulley strain.\n\n**4. Warm Up Thoroughly**\n- Prioritize a long, aerobic warm-up (e.g., hiking to the crag), followed by dynamic stretching and easy climbing before harder moves.\n\n**5. Monitor and Adjust**\n- If pain returns, back off immediately and allow more rest.\n- Full tendon healing can take 100+ days; patience is key.\n\n**6. Avoid Hangboarding or Campus Training**\n- Do not resume finger-intensive training (hangboarding, campus board) until you are climbing pain-free for several weeks.\n\nWould you like specific taping instructions or advice on outdoor-friendly rehab exercises?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:12": -0.02107616886496544, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:6": -0.023152150213718414, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": -0.02428184263408184, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:12": -0.025309843942523003, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": -0.02647622860968113}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbi