{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Here’s how to tape your finger for an A2 pulley injury, tailored for a regular climber like you who prefers not to use supplements:\n\n**Best Taping Method: H-Taping**\n- **Purpose:** Provides extra support to the injured pulley by keeping the tendon close to the bone and reducing load during climbing. Also acts as a psychological reminder to avoid overuse.\n- **Effectiveness:** Research and climbing injury experts agree H-taping is the most supportive method for pulley injuries.\n\n**How to Apply H-Tape:**\n1. **Prepare the Tape:** Cut a strip of climbing tape (about 1.5–2 cm wide). Split each end lengthwise, leaving a 1 cm “bridge” in the middle.\n2. **Placement:** Place the bridge directly over the injured pulley (usually at the base of the finger, over the A2 area).\n3. **Wrap:** With your finger slightly bent (~60°), wrap the split ends around the finger—one set above and one set below the joint—so the tape forms an “H” shape.\n4. **Tension:** The tape should be snug but not cut off circulation.\n5. **Retape Often:** Tape stretches quickly during climbing. Retape between routes for consistent support.\n\n**Alternative: Restrictive Taping**\n- If you want to avoid any risk, use tape to limit finger bending at the injured joint. This prevents crimping entirely, which is safest during early recovery.\n\n**Key Points for You:**\n- Only tape the injured finger; taping healthy fingers is not recommended.\n- Taping is for support during recovery, not prevention.\n- Avoid crimps and high-load moves even when taped.\n\nWould you like a visual guide or video link for H-taping?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": -0.013160980306565762, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": -0.017968494445085526, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": -0.0185443926602602, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": -0.02075442671775818, "clueweb22-en00