{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "The H-taping method is currently considered the most effective taping technique for supporting an injured A2 pulley—especially relevant for climbers like you who want to protect the finger while returning to activity.\n\n**Why H-Taping?**\n- Research shows H-taping provides the best support for keeping the tendon close to the bone, reducing load on the injured pulley, and preventing further injury during flexion (crimping).\n- It outperforms both circular (ring) and X/8 taping in both support and maintaining crimping strength in injured fingers.\n\n**How to Apply H-Taping (per Dr. Schöffl’s method):**\n1. Cut a strip of rigid athletic tape (about 10cm long, 1.5cm wide).\n2. From each end, cut lengthwise toward the center, leaving a 1cm “bridge” in the middle—this creates the “H” shape.\n3. Place the bridge over the crease at the joint above the injured pulley (usually the base of the finger for A2).\n4. Flex your finger to about 60°.\n5. Wrap the upper tabs around the finger above the joint, and the lower tabs below the joint, forming two rings connected by the bridge.\n\n**Key Points:**\n- H-taping restricts harmful movement but allows some function, making it ideal for gradual return.\n- Only use taping on injured fingers; taping healthy fingers can actually increase injury risk.\n- Taping is a support, not a cure—continue to avoid high-stress moves and listen to your pain.\n\nWould you like a visual guide or tips on when to transition away from taping as you heal?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": -0.005730746779590845, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": -0.006448058411478996, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": -0.009648594073951244, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": -0.009815405122935772, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": -0.010243108496069908}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:5": -0.005730746779590845, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": -0.006448058411478996, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": -0.009648594073951244, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": -0.009815405122935772, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": -0.010243108496069908, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:7": -0.012086251750588417, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:11": -0.012365463189780712, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": -0.01406906545162201, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": -0.017335910350084305, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:10": -0.017794840037822723, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": -0.018044831231236458, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": -0.025617923587560654, "clueweb22-en0003-54-05279:1": -0.5782850980758667, "clueweb22-en0007-93-01745:13": -1.0455842018127441}}
{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You asked whether you should tape other (uninjured) fingers when training.\n\n**Direct Answer:**  \nNo, you should not tape healthy fingers as a preventative measure.\n\n**Key Points:**\n\n- **No Benefit for Healthy Fingers:** Research shows that taping uninjured fingers does not provide meaningful support to healthy pulleys. It does not prevent injury and may actually be counterproductive (Schweizer, 2000).\n- **Potential Risks:** Preventative taping can reduce the natural stress that pulleys need to strengthen. This may make your fingers more susceptible to injury over time, as the pulleys won’t adapt to the demands of climbing.\n- **When to Tape:** Only tape the injured finger using the H-taping method or similar, as this provides targeted support to the healing pulley and restricts risky movements (like crimping).\n- **Buddy Taping:** Buddy taping (taping an injured finger to a neighboring finger) is sometimes used for stability in certain injuries, but it’s not recommended for healthy fingers or for pulley injuries unless specifically advised by a medical professional.\n\n**Personalized Justification:**  \nSince you’re recovering from a pulley injury and want to avoid supplements, focusing on proper taping technique for the injured finger and gradual return to climbing is the safest and most effective approach. Taping other fingers could hinder their natural adaptation and increase injury risk.\n\n**Next Step:**  \nWould you like a refresher on how to check if your taping is still effective during a session, or tips on when to stop taping altogether?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": -0.006634589284658432, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:12": -0.014277919195592403, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:1": -0.014547942206263542, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": -0.01688685081899166, "clueweb22-en0002-05-11290:9"