{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Given your background in outdoor climbing, your London location, and your past enjoyment of biking, here are the best alternative activities to boost your mood and energy while your finger heals:\n\n**Top Recommendations**\n\n- **Hiking & Nature Walks**\n  - London offers a wealth of scenic trails, perfect for staying active outdoors. The Epping Forest Oak Trail (6.6 miles, 2–3 hours) is easily accessible from central London and immerses you in nature—ideal for someone who enjoys the outdoors.\n  - Hampstead Heath Circular Walk (4.5 miles, ~2 hours) features ancient woodlands, city views, and swimming ponds. Both are moderate, require no special equipment, and can be done solo or with friends.\n\n- **Cycling**\n  - Since you biked a lot when younger, consider revisiting cycling. London has numerous bike-friendly trails and parks, and cycling is low-impact on your healing finger.\n\n- **Community Sports Clubs**\n  - London has a vibrant community sports scene, including running, netball, and more. These clubs are beginner-friendly and a great way to meet people if you’re missing the social side of climbing.\n\n- **Ice Climbing (Indoor)**\n  - If you want something climbing-adjacent but less stressful on your finger, Vertical Chill in Covent Garden offers an indoor ice wall with routes for all abilities. It’s unique, challenging, and keeps you connected to the climbing community.\n\n**Why These Fit You**\n- All options are outdoors or active, matching your preferences.\n- They’re accessible in London and don’t require full finger strength.\n- They offer a mix of solo and social opportunities.\n\nWould you like more details on any of these—such as specific cycling routes, hiking maps, or how to join a local club?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0009-10-01891:17": -0.041562002152204514, "clueweb22-en0009-10-01891:18": -0