{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Yoga can be as affordable or as premium as you want, especially in London. Here’s a breakdown tailored to your needs:\n\n**Budget Options**\n- **Community Classes:** Some studios offer classes for as little as £5 (e.g., Peckham Levels on Tuesdays, Lumi Power Yoga in Hammersmith on Fridays/Sundays).\n- **Free Classes:** There are occasional free yoga and meditation sessions, such as in Swiss Cottage on Tuesdays.\n- **YouTube & Online:** You can practice entirely for free using YouTube channels. Many people find this effective, especially for maintaining a personal practice at home.\n\n**Mid-Range & Studio Memberships**\n- **Drop-in Classes:** Standard studio classes range from £18–£25 per session.\n- **Intro Offers:** Many studios offer your first class for £10.\n- **Monthly Memberships:** Unlimited classes can cost around £200/month, but some online platforms (like MoreYoga) offer digital memberships for £9.99/month (with a 12-month contract) or £24.99/month for access to a large library of classes.\n\n**Online Platforms**\n- **MoreYoga:** £9.99/month for live and on-demand classes (12-month contract).\n- **East of Eden:** £24.99/month for digital membership, with a 14-day free trial.\n\n**What fits you best?**\n- If you want to keep costs low and flexibility high, start with free YouTube classes or community sessions.\n- If you prefer structure and variety, consider an online membership.\n- For in-person community and motivation, look for local £5–£10 classes.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific YouTube channels or help finding a nearby community class?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0020-01-05276:9": -0.019612791016697884, "clueweb22-en0023-59-04411:2": -0.021056674420833588, "clueweb22-en0020-01-05276:8": -0.024122780188918114, "clueweb22-en0019-90-08199:5": -0.027981305494904518, "clueweb22-en0026-11-