{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To prepare for a 10-mile run as a 22-year-old beginner with low fitness confidence, focus on gradual, structured training and essential gear. Here’s a tailored approach:\n\n**Training Structure**\n- **Start Slow & Build Gradually:** Begin by running or run/walking 20–30 minutes, 3 times per week. Once you can run 10 minutes nonstop, increase by 2–5 minutes each week (e.g., 10 → 12 → 15 minutes).\n- **Progressive Mileage:** Plan for 8–10 weeks of training. Each week, slightly increase your total distance, aiming to build endurance safely.\n- **Rest & Recovery:** Include at least 1–2 rest days per week. Cross-training (cycling, swimming, or strength exercises) 1–2 times weekly helps prevent injury and boosts fitness.\n- **Intervals & Sprints:** Once weekly, add short sprints (e.g., 2-minute fast runs with 2-minute jog recoveries) to build speed and stamina, but only after you’re comfortable with steady running.\n\n**Gear & Essentials**\n- **Proper Shoes & Clothing:** Invest in comfortable running shoes and moisture-wicking clothes suitable for Spain’s climate.\n- **Hydration & Fuel:** For runs over 60 minutes, carry water and experiment with simple mid-run snacks (like energy gels or fruit) during training—not on race day.\n\n**Mindset & Goals**\n- **Set Realistic Targets:** Focus on finishing, not speed. Consistency is more important than intensity.\n- **Track Progress:** Use a simple log or app to monitor your runs and improvements.\n\nWould you like a sample week-by-week beginner training plan, or tips on choosing the right running gear for Spain’s weather?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0008-64-18271:10": -0.2591263949871063, "clueweb22-en0034-56-03500:13": -0.2918969690799713, "clueweb22-en0013-30-04535:7": -0.3629167675971985, "clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:4": -0.3695259094238281, "clueweb22-en0005-57-00569:1": -0.