{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "With four months to prepare for your 10km run in New York next April, you have ample time to build fitness, minimize injury risk, and peak on race day. Here’s how to structure your training for maximum benefit:\n\n**Optimal Weekly Structure (12–16 Weeks Out)**\n- **3–4 Runs per Week:** \n  - 1 long run (gradually increasing distance)\n  - 1 interval/speed session (e.g., 5 x 1km at 10k pace, 2 min rest)\n  - 1 tempo run (e.g., 2 x 10 min at threshold pace, 3 min rest)\n  - 1–2 easy/recovery runs (40–50 min, relaxed pace)\n- **2 Rest Days per Week:** Essential for recovery and injury prevention.\n- **Active Recovery Week:** Every 4th week, cut volume by half to allow your body to adapt and reduce injury risk.\n\n**Strength & Conditioning**\n- **2 x 40-min sessions/week:** Focus on core, glutes, and flexibility. Do these in addition to running, not instead of.\n\n**Progression & Mileage**\n- Increase weekly mileage by no more than 10% to avoid overtraining.\n- Peak at 15–20 miles/week by the final month.\n\n**Personalization for Your Timeline**\n- With 16 weeks, start with lower mileage and build gradually.\n- Use the first 4 weeks for base conditioning (easy runs, light strength).\n- Shift to more structured workouts (intervals, tempo) in weeks 5–12.\n- Taper in the final 2 weeks: reduce volume, keep intensity.\n\n**Weather Consideration**\n- April in NYC is cool and ideal for running, but be prepared for rain.\n\nWould you like a detailed 16-week plan, or tips on how to adjust if you miss a week?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0004-91-11430:5": -0.14908774197101593, "clueweb22-en0032-17-06111:4": -0.15177655220031738, "clueweb22-en0033-30-18644:7": -0.20463919639587402, "clueweb22-en0010-76-19904:10": -0.2271280586719513, "clueweb22-en0005-33-13481:0": -0.26679521799087524}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am not very confide