{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re planning for a 10km run in April, with a two-week vacation in January. Here’s how to keep your training on track and flexible:\n\n**How to Adjust Your Training for Vacation**\n- **Flexibility is Key:** If you’re away or have less time, shift workouts as needed. For example, move a key session earlier in the week or double up harder days if necessary, but add extra rest after (clueweb22-en0040-47-04859, clueweb22-en0022-92-01169).\n- **Consistency Over Perfection:** Missing or shortening a few runs won’t derail your progress if you stay consistent overall.\n- **Cross-Training Counts:** If running isn’t possible, substitute with cross-training (cycling, swimming, brisk walking, or even snowshoeing if you’re in a winter spot). This maintains endurance and keeps you active (clueweb22-en0030-93-00475).\n- **Vacation as Active Recovery:** If you can’t train as usual, treat the vacation as a recovery period. You’ll return fresher and ready to ramp up.\n\n**Sample Adjusted Week (During Vacation)**\n- 2–3 easy runs (30–40 min, effort-based)\n- 1 cross-training session (bike, swim, hike)\n- 2–3 rest or active recovery days\n\n**Post-Vacation**\n- Resume your regular plan. If you missed key workouts, don’t try to “make up” all at once—just continue as scheduled.\n\n**Why This Works for You**\n- Keeps training enjoyable and stress-free during travel.\n- Prevents burnout and injury by allowing flexibility.\n- Maintains your fitness base for a strong build-up to race day.\n\nWould you like a detailed two-week vacation training template, or tips for running in unfamiliar locations?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0040-47-04859:16": -0.1358141154050827, "clueweb22-en0042-72-11730:7": -0.16939550638198853, "clueweb22-en0030-93-00475:2": -0.19842301309108734, "clueweb22-en0030-93-00475:1": -0.1984865516424179, "clueweb22-en002