{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-1_12", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Switching from a 10K to a 5K will change your training, but not dramatically—especially with your long timeline. Here’s how the plan would shift, tailored to your goals and preferences:\n\n**Key Differences: 10K vs. 5K Training**\n- **Volume:** 5K plans involve less weekly mileage and shorter long runs (typically maxing out at 5–6 km).\n- **Intensity:** 5K training emphasizes more speedwork (intervals, repetitions) and less focus on long, steady endurance runs.\n- **Recovery:** Slightly more rest or easy days, as the intensity of speedwork is higher.\n\n**Sample 5K Week (Intermediate Level)**\n- **Monday:** Rest or cross-training (e.g., cycling, swimming)\n- **Tuesday:** Intervals (e.g., 5 x 400m at 5K pace, with full recovery)\n- **Wednesday:** Easy run (20–30 min)\n- **Thursday:** Tempo run (15–20 min at “comfortably hard” pace)\n- **Friday:** Rest or easy run (20 min)\n- **Saturday:** Long run (max 5–6 km, easy pace)\n- **Sunday:** Rest\n\n**Other Considerations**\n- You can easily adjust for vacations or busy weeks—just shift workouts or add extra rest as needed.\n- Cross-training and strength work remain valuable for injury prevention and overall fitness.\n- If you’re new to running, a run-walk approach is effective for 5K prep.\n\n**Personalized Advice**\nGiven your ample prep time, you can start with a base-building phase (easy runs, cross-training), then shift to more focused 5K work 8 weeks out. This keeps training fresh and motivation high.\n\nWould you like a detailed 8-week 5K plan, or tips on how to transition from your current routine?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0042-72-11730:7": -0.07014329731464386, "clueweb22-en0022-92-01169:16": -0.08008576929569244, "clueweb22-en0005-48-11149:1": -0.12036796659231186, "clueweb22-en0019-16-15604:1": -0.13372772932052612, "clueweb22-en0022-10-14457:8": -0.14291