{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "4-2_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "As a primary school history teacher visiting New York, you’ll find a wealth of museums beyond the Metropolitan Museum of Art that are both engaging and educational for your interests. Here are top recommendations tailored to your background:\n\n**Best Museums for History Teachers**\n\n- **American Museum of Natural History**  \n  - Renowned for its dinosaur skeletons, human history exhibits, and interactive science displays.  \n  - Offers age-appropriate, hands-on learning experiences ideal for educators and students.\n\n- **New-York Historical Society**  \n  - Focuses on the city’s and nation’s history, featuring artifacts, documents, and immersive exhibits.  \n  - Excellent for understanding American history and New York’s role in it.\n\n- **Museum of the City of New York**  \n  - Explores NYC’s evolution through engaging exhibits on immigration, urban development, and social change.  \n  - Great for teaching about city life and diversity.\n\n- **Tenement Museum**  \n  - Brings immigrant history to life with guided tours of restored apartments and stories of real families.  \n  - Especially relevant for lessons on immigration and social history.\n\n**Other Notable Mentions**\n- **Museum of the Moving Image** (film/media history)\n- **New York Transit Museum** (history of public transportation)\n- **Skyscraper Museum** (architecture and urban history)\n\nThese museums offer rich, curriculum-relevant content and interactive experiences that can inspire both you and your students.\n\nWould you like tips on planning your visits, or recommendations for specific exhibits or educator resources at these museums?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0024-85-02817:1": -0.045381058007478714, "clueweb22-en0039-85-09291:2": -0.0455763004720211, "clueweb22-en0024-85-02817:0": -0.05078902095556259, "clueweb22-en0029-28-19159:10": -0.0