{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Chia jam is a creative, healthier breakfast option, but you’re right to ask about its calcium content compared to milk and other foods.\n\n**Calcium Content per 100g:**\n- **Chia Seeds:** ~631 mg calcium per 100g (the main source of calcium in chia jam)\n- **Chia Jam:** ~18–21 mg calcium per 2-tbsp serving; if you scale up, a 100g portion of chia jam (depending on recipe, but assuming a high chia seed ratio) could provide roughly 120–150 mg calcium—still less than pure chia seeds due to dilution with fruit.\n- **Cow’s Milk:** ~120 mg calcium per 100g (or 275–300 mg per cup/240 ml)\n- **Sesame Seeds:** ~1000 mg calcium per 100g (highest among common seeds)\n- **Spinach (cooked):** ~150 mg calcium per 100g\n\n**Key Takeaways for Your Breakfast Choices:**\n- **Milk** remains the most concentrated, easily absorbed source of calcium per serving.\n- **Chia jam** offers some calcium, but you’d need a large portion to match a glass of milk.\n- **Chia seeds** and **sesame seeds** are excellent plant-based calcium sources—consider adding them to other meals if you’re avoiding dairy.\n- **Leafy greens** (like spinach, broccoli) and **fortified plant milks** are also strong alternatives.\n\n**Personalized Suggestion:**  \nIf you dislike milk, boost your breakfast’s calcium by adding extra chia or sesame seeds, or pair chia jam with whole grain bread and a side of leafy greens or fortified plant milk.\n\nWould you like ideas for high-calcium, dairy-free breakfasts?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0023-32-19489:1": -0.15967948734760284, "clueweb22-en0034-05-15888:7": -0.26214778423309326, "clueweb22-en0021-94-19676:10": -0.5540844202041626, "clueweb22-en0004-95-09693:1": -0.5616874694824219, "clueweb22-en0041-21-11359:5": -0.6317763328552246}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it.", "I