{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Breastfeeding during pregnancy is generally safe for both you and your baby—there is no evidence that it is \"bad\" or harmful in most cases.\n\n**Key Points:**\n\n- **Safety:** Most experts and health organizations agree that breastfeeding while pregnant is safe for healthy women with uncomplicated pregnancies. Your body will continue to provide enough nutrients for both your nursing child and your developing baby.\n- **Mild Contractions:** Breastfeeding releases small amounts of oxytocin, which can cause mild uterine contractions. In healthy pregnancies, these are not strong enough to cause miscarriage or preterm labor.\n- **When to Be Cautious:** If you have a high-risk pregnancy, a history of miscarriage or preterm labor, are carrying multiples, or experience uterine pain or bleeding, consult your doctor. In these cases, your provider may recommend weaning.\n- **Benefits:** Breastfeeding offers significant health benefits for both mother and child, including reduced risk of certain cancers for you and better immunity and nutrition for your child.\n- **Mental Health:** Continuing to breastfeed may even help reduce the risk of postpartum depression.\n\n**What to Do Next:**  \nCheck in with your healthcare provider to discuss your specific situation, especially if you have any risk factors.\n\nWould you like tips on managing breastfeeding while pregnant, or information about weaning if needed?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-14-02860:1": -0.07930300384759903, "clueweb22-en0040-60-11237:16": -0.09352469444274902, "clueweb22-en0046-45-08609:0": -0.09809181839227676, "clueweb22-en0005-46-00778:0": -0.10069779306650162, "clueweb22-en0017-26-01032:0": -0.10084890574216843}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am a woman.", "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it.", "I am 25 years old."]}], "references": {"