{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Breastfeeding does not require a special or restrictive diet. You can continue to enjoy your usual foods—jam, white rice, cake, and orange juice—while breastfeeding. Here’s what matters most for you:\n\n**Balanced Diet is Key**\n- Eating a variety of foods (fruits, vegetables, protein, whole grains, dairy) supports your energy and health, which in turn benefits your baby.\n- There’s no evidence that eating specific foods like jam, white rice, or cake harms your milk or your baby. Occasional treats are fine.\n\n**Carbohydrates & Low-Carb Diets**\n- If you’re interested in low-carb eating, be cautious: breastfeeding requires a minimum of about 210g of carbs per day to maintain milk supply and energy.\n- Extreme low-carb or ketogenic diets are not recommended while breastfeeding, especially in the early months. If you want to reduce carbs, do so gradually and don’t go below 1800 calories per day.\n- Choose healthier carb sources when possible (whole grains, fruits, legumes), but your current staples (rice, cake, jam) are not off-limits.\n\n**Foods to Avoid**\n- Unlike pregnancy, there are no strict foods to avoid while breastfeeding. Only restrict foods if you notice your baby reacting poorly (e.g., fussiness after you eat dairy).\n\n**Personalized Tips**\n- Your breakfast jam, white rice lunches, and cake snacks are all compatible with breastfeeding. Just balance them with nutrient-rich foods for overall health.\n- Fresh orange juice is a good source of folate and vitamin C.\n\nWould you like meal ideas that fit your preferences and support breastfeeding?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0002-45-09980:0": -0.0326482318341732, "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:2": -0.06366994231939316, "clueweb22-en0002-45-09980:4": -0.08779525756835938, "clueweb22-en0011-53-14159:11": -0.10291247069835663, "clueweb22-en0018-51-03723:6": -0.105