{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You are unlikely to \"run out\" of milk for your baby. Most women are able to produce enough breast milk, and true low supply is rare. Here’s what matters most for you:\n\n**What Actually Causes Low Milk Supply?**\n- The most common causes are infrequent feeding, poor latch, or supplementing with formula without expressing milk. Feeding on demand (8–12 times/day for newborns) is key.\n- Medical factors like retained placenta, postpartum hemorrhage, certain health conditions (e.g., diabetes, thyroid issues), or previous breast surgery can affect supply, but these are less common.\n- Some medications (especially those with estrogen), smoking, and excessive alcohol can reduce supply.\n\n**Normal Baby Behavior vs. Low Supply**\n- Babies often want to feed frequently, especially during growth spurts or in the evenings. This is normal and helps increase your supply.\n- Signs your baby is getting enough: plenty of wet nappies, steady weight gain, and contentment after feeds.\n- Soft breasts, less leaking, or not feeling a \"let-down\" do NOT mean you have low supply.\n\n**What You Can Do**\n- Feed on demand and ensure a good latch.\n- Stay hydrated and eat a balanced diet.\n- Avoid unnecessary formula supplementation unless advised by a healthcare provider.\n\n**When to Seek Help**\n- If your baby isn’t gaining weight, has few wet nappies, or you have concerns, consult a lactation consultant or your doctor.\n\nWould you like tips on boosting supply or signs your baby is getting enough milk?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0045-75-07525:1": -0.018272286280989647, "clueweb22-en0037-76-04586:3": -0.018634844571352005, "clueweb22-en0021-55-01275:1": -0.019470402970910072, "clueweb22-en0035-54-11093:6": -0.019902529194951057, "clueweb22-en0019-48-05136:2": -0.023011546581983566}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I usually eat white rice for