{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Congratulations again on your pregnancy and excitement! Here’s what you need to know right now as you prepare for breastfeeding and newborn care:\n\n**Breastfeeding Essentials**\n- **Start Early:** Begin breastfeeding within the first hour after birth. This helps establish milk supply and provides your baby with colostrum, which is rich in nutrients and antibodies.\n- **Exclusive Feeding:** Breast milk alone is recommended for the first 6 months—no water, formula, or other foods unless medically advised.\n- **Responsive Feeding:** Feed your baby whenever they show hunger cues, not on a strict schedule. This helps maintain your milk supply.\n- **Skin-to-Skin Contact:** Keep your baby close and have as much skin-to-skin time as possible, especially in the early days. This supports bonding and milk production.\n- **Support:** Breastfeeding can take several weeks to feel natural. If you have trouble, reach out to a lactation consultant, your midwife, or the National Breastfeeding Helpline (1800 686 268, 24/7).\n\n**Self-Care & Recovery**\n- **Rest:** Some days, just feeding and comforting your baby is enough. Prioritize rest and don’t expect perfection.\n- **Hydration & Nutrition:** Drink plenty of fluids and eat a balanced diet. No need for a special diet, but avoid strict low-carb plans while breastfeeding.\n\n**Newborn Care Basics**\n- Learn about safe sleeping, nappy changes, and bathing.\n- Prepare for your first pediatrician visit and expect changes in your baby’s feeding and bowel habits in the first weeks.\n\nWould you like practical tips for the first days at home, or more on what to expect with newborn routines?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0044-58-01018:9": -0.03643865883350372, "clueweb22-en0002-16-03599:2": -0.03724179416894913, "clueweb22-en0021-49-11932:18": -0.03734366223216057, "clueweb22-en0002-06-10