{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "5-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Congratulations again on your pregnancy and your excitement about being a caring mum! Here’s what you need to know about newborn crying, tailored to your concerns:\n\n**Crying Is Normal and Healthy**\n- Newborns typically cry 2–3 hours a day, especially in the first few months. Crying often peaks around 6–8 weeks of age.\n- Crying is your baby’s only way to communicate needs—hunger, tiredness, discomfort, or needing a diaper change. It is not manipulation or a sign you’re doing something wrong.\n\n**What You Can Do**\n- Responding quickly and lovingly to your baby’s cries (ideally within 90 seconds) can actually reduce overall crying and helps your baby feel secure.\n- Feeding on demand, especially if breastfeeding, and keeping your baby close (holding or using a carrier) can help soothe them and may decrease crying.\n- Babies often cry more in the late afternoon and early evening—this is normal.\n\n**When to Seek Help**\n- If your baby’s cry sounds different, or if you’re worried for any reason, check with your doctor.\n- As long as your baby is feeding well, has plenty of wet nappies, and is gaining weight, crying is rarely a sign of a serious problem.\n\n**Reassurance**\n- You cannot spoil a newborn by comforting them. Holding, cuddling, and responding to their needs builds trust and attachment.\n\nWould you like tips on how to soothe a crying baby, or information about sleep routines for newborns?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0011-17-01403:7": -0.058022309094667435, "clueweb22-en0038-57-04444:0": -0.0691649466753006, "clueweb22-en0043-40-01751:2": -0.07245022058486938, "clueweb22-en0022-43-13342:10": -0.11478219926357269, "clueweb22-en0006-80-13155:3": -0.12300115078687668}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am a woman."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0011-17-01403:7": -0.058022309094667435, "clueweb22-en0038-57-04444: