{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-2_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For oily hair, ingredient selection is crucial. Here’s what to look for—and what to avoid—based on the latest evidence and your needs:\n\n**Best Ingredients for Oily Hair:**\n- **Oil-Controlling Agents:** Tea tree oil, peppermint, and rosemary help cleanse and balance scalp oil without over-drying (Paul Mitchell Tea Tree Special Shampoo, Everyone Hair Sulfate-Free Shampoo).\n- **Gentle Detergents:** Sodium lauryl sulfate, ammonium-based detergents, or sulfosuccinates effectively remove excess oil. These are especially helpful if your scalp feels greasy soon after washing.\n- **Natural Extracts:** Moringa seed oil, sunflower seed extract, turmeric, and quinoa protein offer gentle cleansing and scalp health benefits without heavy residue (Verb Ghost Shampoo, Seed Phytonutrients Daily Hair Cleanser).\n\n**What to Avoid:**\n- **Heavy Moisturizers:** Skip shampoos labeled “moisturizing,” “hydrating,” or “for curly/frizzy hair”—these can weigh hair down and worsen oiliness.\n- **Harsh Chemicals:** Avoid parabens and sulfates if you prefer a gentler, more natural approach, though some sulfates can help with oil control if your scalp is very oily.\n- **Oils in Conditioners:** If you use conditioner, choose one formulated for oily hair and avoid added oils.\n\n**Extra Tips:**\n- Use clarifying shampoos occasionally for deep cleansing, but not daily to prevent over-drying.\n- Consider sulfate-free, natural ingredient formulas if you want to minimize chemical exposure.\n\nWould you like specific product recommendations tailored to your hair type or sensitivities?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0020-53-05239:1": -0.013271682895720005, "clueweb22-en0037-41-10440:4": -0.013720135204494, "clueweb22-en0020-53-05239:15": -0.018848596140742302, "clueweb22-en0018-54-01822:10": -0.024584654718637466, "clueweb22-en0014-71-05022:3": -0.0