{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-2_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For oily hair—especially if you use heat styling tools—your shampoo should both control oil and protect against heat-related damage. Here’s how to optimize your choice:\n\n**Key Ingredients to Look For**\n- **Niacinamide**: Supports scalp health, regulates oil, and soothes irritation.\n- **Lactic Acid or LHA**: Gentle exfoliants that help clear pores and reduce oiliness without over-drying.\n- **Salicylic Acid**: Deeply cleanses and breaks down scalp oil.\n- **Tea Tree Oil**: Dissolves oil and debris, helps with dandruff and scalp buildup.\n- **Zinc Pyrithione**: Controls dandruff and excess oil.\n- **Panthenol & Hydrolyzed Keratin**: Strengthen hair, which is crucial if you heat style often.\n\n**Formulation Tips**\n- Choose shampoos labeled “balancing,” “clarifying,” or “for oily hair.”\n- Avoid heavy moisturizing or smoothing shampoos—they can weigh hair down and worsen oiliness.\n- Look for formulas free from silicones, parabens, and artificial fragrances, which can cause buildup and irritation.\n\n**Extra Tips for Oily, Heat-Styled Hair**\n- Limit touching your hair to reduce oil transfer.\n- Use a clarifying shampoo once a week to remove product and oil buildup, but don’t overuse to avoid dryness.\n- After washing, use a heat protectant spray before styling to shield hair from damage.\n\nWould you like specific product recommendations that match these criteria, or tips on building a full hair care routine for oily, heat-styled hair?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0018-54-01822:9": -0.20236274600028992, "clueweb22-en0031-86-16386:16": -0.2437644898891449, "clueweb22-en0029-13-07561:5": -0.26031798124313354, "clueweb22-en0045-07-15818:3": -0.2831089496612549, "clueweb22-en0032-06-16827:11": -0.29685577750205994}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm allergic to nuts, so I have to be careful about ingredients.", "I frequentl