{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "6-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For hair that is oily at the scalp but dry at the ends, you need a shampoo that deeply cleanses without stripping moisture from your lengths. Here’s what stands out from the research:\n\n**Top Recommendations**\n\n- **Mamaearth Bhringamla Shampoo**\n  - Specifically formulated for oily scalps and dry ends.\n  - Contains bhringraj and amla to strengthen hair and prevent dandruff and hair fall.\n  - Sulphate- and paraben-free, so it won’t overdry your hair.\n\n- **Shampoos with Balanced pH**\n  - Look for shampoos that correct scalp pH (around 5.5). These cleanse excess oil from the scalp while protecting hair moisture.\n  - Example: Sebamed Everyday Shampoo (soap- and alkali-free, pH 5.5) is gentle, retains volume, and is suitable for sensitive scalps.\n\n- **Paul Mitchell Tea Tree Special Shampoo**\n  - Tea tree oil, peppermint, and lavender invigorate the scalp and remove oil without weighing hair down.\n  - Leaves hair feeling clean and refreshed.\n\n**Key Ingredients to Seek**\n- Tea tree oil, salicylic acid, or LHA for oil control and scalp clarity.\n- Mild, sulfate-free cleansers to avoid over-drying.\n- Natural moisturizers (like aloe vera or honey) for dry ends.\n\n**How to Use**\n- Focus shampoo on the scalp; condition only the mid-lengths and ends.\n- Avoid heavy, creamy shampoos or those labeled “moisturizing” for the scalp.\n\nWould you like product links or tips on how to alternate between clarifying and moisturizing products for best results?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0020-53-05239:1": -0.009394722990691662, "clueweb22-en0014-37-09933:1": -0.01741815358400345, "clueweb22-en0025-00-10438:8": -0.01852484978735447, "clueweb22-en0012-95-06097:3": -0.018936915323138237, "clueweb22-en0021-47-11651:9": -0.021703358739614487}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm allergic to nuts, so I have to be careful about ingredi