{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Feeling overwhelmed by too many tasks is a common ADHD challenge, especially in an office setting. Here’s how you can manage this effectively:\n\n**1. Prioritize and Break Down Tasks**\n- Use a daily checklist or to-do list, posted somewhere visible (physical or digital). This helps make tasks concrete and reduces mental clutter.\n- Break large projects into smaller, manageable steps. Focus on completing one small goal at a time rather than tackling everything at once.\n\n**2. Smart Scheduling**\n- Draw up a simple schedule for your day. Block out time for specific tasks, and stick to it as much as possible.\n- Avoid overscheduling—say no to extra projects if your plate is already full.\n\n**3. Manage Overwhelm with Breaks**\n- Take short, regular breaks between tasks. Even a quick walk or a few minutes of meditation can reset your focus and reduce stress.\n\n**4. Organize Your Workspace**\n- Keep your desk tidy. Physical clutter can add to feelings of overwhelm and make it harder to focus.\n\n**5. Advocate and Delegate**\n- If possible, delegate tasks that aren’t essential for you to complete personally.\n- Communicate your workload and boundaries to colleagues or supervisors when needed.\n\n**6. Reward Progress**\n- Celebrate small wins. Treat yourself when you finish a task to reinforce positive habits.\n\nThese strategies are tailored for your quiet office environment and focus on reducing overwhelm, not just distractions. Would you like specific tools or templates for checklists and schedules to get started?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0001-03-06965:6": -0.0056531080044806, "clueweb22-en0024-69-14725:7": -0.005875459406524897, "clueweb22-en0007-35-00036:3": -0.00597369996830821, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:6": -0.007339774165302515, "clueweb22-en0001-03-06965:5": -0.00784825999289751}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I 