{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Breaking down tasks doesn’t have to mean creating an endless list of new things to do—in fact, for ADHD brains, over-planning can make overwhelm worse. Here’s a more effective, office-friendly approach:\n\n**Focus on Just the Next Step**\n- Instead of mapping out every detail, identify only the first small action you need to take. For example: “Open the project file,” or “Send a quick update email.” This keeps things simple and actionable, reducing mental clutter.\n\n**Use Quick Checklists for Repeated Tasks**\n- For tasks you do regularly, create a short checklist. This saves mental energy and helps you start without overthinking the process.\n\n**Prioritize by Ease or Interest**\n- When feeling stuck, pick the task that feels easiest or most interesting right now. This builds momentum and makes the workload feel less daunting.\n\n**Avoid Overcommitting**\n- If your workload is consistently unmanageable, consider discussing priorities with your manager. Setting clear boundaries can prevent burnout.\n\n**Why This Works for You**\n- These strategies are tailored for quiet office environments and help prevent the “analysis paralysis” that comes from seeing the whole mountain at once.\n\nWould you like a template for a simple daily checklist, or tips on how to talk to your manager about workload?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0030-53-01118:15": -0.011135691776871681, "clueweb22-en0021-66-19608:8": -0.01286116149276495, "clueweb22-en0041-10-01075:17": -0.013134037144482136, "clueweb22-en0007-85-19058:8": -0.013733774423599243, "clueweb22-en0040-38-17418:12": -0.015567801892757416}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I work in an office.", "I live in Germany.", "I love it when it is quiet."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0030-53-01118:15": -0.011135691776871681, "clueweb22-en0021-66-19608:8": -0.01286116149276495, "clueweb22-en0041-