{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re experiencing overwhelm and task-switching—common ADHD challenges—especially when juggling multiple tasks. Here’s how to regain focus and finish what you start:\n\n**1. Monotasking Over Multitasking**\n- Focus on one task at a time. Multitasking increases overwhelm and reduces completion rates for people with ADHD.\n- Limit your daily to-do list to 3–4 items. Hide the rest until you finish or reach a natural stopping point.\n\n**2. Structured Scheduling**\n- Instead of a general to-do list, schedule specific tasks into your calendar. This reduces the mental load and makes each task feel more manageable.\n- If you must switch, pick just two tasks to alternate between. This channels your natural tendency to shift focus without losing track of progress.\n\n**3. Learn Your Personal Rhythm**\n- Many with ADHD work best by accepting their unique workflow—sometimes starting several tasks and finishing them in a non-linear order. Plan for this by allowing buffer time and not comparing yourself to others’ work styles.\n\n**4. Physical Tools and Environment**\n- Consider subtle fidget tools (e.g., rings, stress balls) if movement helps you focus.\n- Keep your workspace organized to minimize visual overwhelm.\n\n**Why this works for you:**  \nThese strategies directly address your feeling of overwhelm and tendency to jump between tasks, helping you finish more and feel less scattered.\n\nWould you like a sample daily schedule or tips on how to prioritize your top 3–4 tasks?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:5": -0.014943327754735947, "clueweb22-en0021-33-10264:5": -0.015928760170936584, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:4": -0.016682643443346024, "clueweb22-en0007-35-00036:2": -0.019102534279227257, "clueweb22-en0020-61-13551:10": -0.020753026008605957}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I live in Germany.", "I work in an office.