{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To address your challenge—difficulty focusing on one task and jumping between tasks—here’s a targeted approach that works with your ADHD, not against it, and fits your office, sedentary environment:\n\n**Top Strategy: Timed Task Switching**\n\n- **How it Works:**  \n  - Pick two priority tasks.\n  - Set a timer (e.g., 20–25 minutes) to focus on just one task.\n  - When the timer goes off, take a short break (5 minutes), then switch to the other task for the next timed session.\n  - Repeat this cycle.  \n  - This leverages your natural tendency to shift focus, but in a controlled, productive way.\n\n**Why This Helps:**\n- Prevents overwhelm by narrowing your focus to just one task at a time.\n- Gives you permission to switch, reducing guilt or paralysis.\n- Timers provide external structure and help you track progress, which is especially useful in a sedentary office setting.\n\n**Practical Tips:**\n- Use a simple phone timer or a visual timer app (some show time passing with a colored disk, which can be more motivating).\n- Schedule these focused sessions into your calendar to create routine and predictability.\n- Reward yourself after a few cycles—small incentives can boost motivation.\n\n**Personalized Justification:**  \nThis method is ideal for quiet office work and doesn’t require physical activity. It’s designed to help you finish tasks without feeling stuck or overwhelmed.\n\nWould you like recommendations for timer apps or more ideas on how to prioritize which two tasks to start with?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0042-41-15251:8": -0.04147256910800934, "clueweb22-en0035-93-06191:4": -0.06055202707648277, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:3": -0.062080103904008865, "clueweb22-en0026-06-01592:6": -0.06587041169404984, "clueweb22-en0017-11-11118:12": -0.06614046543836594}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary lif