{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_6", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re looking for ADHD-friendly work strategies that don’t interrupt your hyperfocus or feel too rigid—unlike the Pomodoro Technique. Here’s what works best for your needs:\n\n**1. Flowtime Technique: Flexible Focus**\n- Instead of fixed intervals, you start working and only note the time when you begin and when you naturally lose focus or want a break.\n- Take a break when you feel your attention fading, not when a timer tells you.\n- This lets you ride out periods of hyperfocus without interruption, but still encourages regular breaks to prevent burnout.\n- You can track your focus patterns over time to better understand your natural rhythms.\n\n**2. Gentle Transitions Out of Hyperfocus**\n- If you struggle to stop after a long period of focus, set a “pre-transition” reminder 10–15 minutes before you need to switch tasks.\n- Use a visual timer or a subtle phone alert as a cue to start winding down, rather than a hard stop.\n- Physical movement (standing up, changing seats) can help break the hyperfocus “trance” if you find yourself ignoring timers.\n\n**3. Task Switching—But With Structure**\n- If you need variety, pick two tasks and allow yourself to switch between them when your attention wanes, rather than forcing yourself to stick to one.\n- Scheduling just one or two key tasks per day (instead of a long to-do list) can reduce overwhelm.\n\nWould you like a quick guide on setting up the Flowtime Technique, or tips for tracking your focus patterns?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-55-03763:1": -0.015709219500422478, "clueweb22-en0036-02-14625:4": -0.030279209837317467, "clueweb22-en0039-55-00787:11": -0.03208143636584282, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:7": -0.03395739942789078, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": -0.033981941640377045}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary lifestyle.", "I live in Germany.", "I wo