{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To help you protect your hyperfocus from interruptions like emails or meetings in your office setting, here are the most effective, ADHD-friendly strategies:\n\n**1. Proactively Manage Digital Distractions**\n- Silence non-urgent notifications (email, chat, apps) before starting deep work. Consider using \"Do Not Disturb\" mode or pausing notifications entirely.\n- Close unnecessary browser tabs and programs. Only keep open what you need for the current task.\n\n**2. Handle Scheduled Interruptions**\n- Block out \"focus time\" on your calendar so colleagues know not to schedule meetings during those periods.\n- If a meeting is unavoidable, set a 5-minute reminder before it starts so you can wrap up your current thought and transition smoothly.\n\n**3. Capture, Don’t React**\n- Keep a notepad or digital sticky note handy. If a distracting thought or task pops up (like remembering an email you need to send), jot it down immediately and return to your main task. This prevents mental clutter without breaking your flow.\n\n**4. Physical Environment Tweaks**\n- Use noise-cancelling headphones or white noise to signal to others (and yourself) that you’re in focus mode.\n- If possible, work in a less-trafficked area or close your office door during deep work sessions.\n\n**5. Batch Responses**\n- Route calls and emails to voicemail or inbox, and check them only at set times (e.g., after a focus block or during a natural break).\n\nThese approaches let you leverage your hyperfocus while minimizing disruptions, tailored to your quiet office environment. Would you like tips on communicating these boundaries to your team, or help setting up digital tools to automate some of these steps?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-60-15663:0": -0.006873771082609892, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": -0.0072778817266225815, "clueweb22-en001