{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To help you manage meetings with ADHD in an office setting, here are the most effective, actionable strategies:\n\n**1. Prepare and Capture Key Points**\n- Bring a notepad or digital device to jot down important details and action items. This helps channel restlessness and prevents forgetfulness.\n- Make a brief agenda or checklist before the meeting so you know what to focus on.\n\n**2. Minimize Interruptions to Your Flow**\n- If you’re in hyperfocus before a meeting, set a gentle reminder 10–15 minutes beforehand. This gives your brain time to transition, reducing the jarring effect of being pulled out of deep work.\n- If timers aren’t enough, consider asking a colleague to give you a heads-up before meetings.\n\n**3. Batch and Buffer**\n- Try to schedule meetings back-to-back or in specific blocks, leaving larger chunks of uninterrupted time for focused work.\n- After meetings, take a short break to reset before diving back into tasks.\n\n**4. Avoid Over-Scheduling**\n- Be mindful not to stack too many meetings in one day. If possible, work with your supervisor to keep your schedule manageable.\n\n**5. Self-Reward and Self-Care**\n- Give yourself a small reward after attending and participating in meetings—this can help with motivation and follow-through.\n\nThese strategies are tailored to your office environment and ADHD needs. Would you like tips on how to communicate these preferences to your team or manager, or help with post-meeting task follow-up?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:11": -0.014359239488840103, "clueweb22-en0019-85-03855:10": -0.01948513463139534, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:7": -0.04217807576060295, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:1": -0.04249265789985657, "clueweb22-en0013-60-10409:8": -0.056249819695949554}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary lifestyle.", "I live in Germany.", "I