{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You’re asking how to handle meetings as a disruptive force—especially when they break your hyperfocus or derail your workflow. Here’s how to minimize their impact, tailored for ADHD in an office setting:\n\n**Batch and Block Meetings**\n- If possible, cluster meetings into specific days or time blocks (e.g., all meetings on Mondays and Fridays). This leaves other days open for deep, uninterrupted work. This approach is especially effective for ADHD brains, as it reduces unpredictable interruptions and helps you mentally compartmentalize (clueweb22-en0006-62-03234).\n\n**Protect Focus Time**\n- Use your calendar to schedule “focus blocks” where no meetings can be booked. Treat these as non-negotiable appointments with yourself (clueweb22-en0006-19-19181).\n- Plan your most demanding tasks for these protected periods.\n\n**Transition Strategies**\n- Before a meeting, jot down exactly where you are in your current task. This makes it easier to pick up where you left off after the interruption (clueweb22-en0030-54-15087).\n- After the meeting, take a minute to review your notes and re-orient yourself before diving back in.\n\n**Leverage Tech**\n- Set reminders for meetings so you’re not caught off guard, but also so you can wrap up your current focus session with intention (clueweb22-en0038-88-03812).\n\n**Personalization**\nThese strategies work because they reduce the number of transitions—something that’s particularly challenging with ADHD. By batching meetings and protecting focus time, you’re less likely to lose momentum or feel scattered.\n\nWould you like tips on how to negotiate meeting times with your team, or tools to help automate this process?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0006-62-03234:3": -0.008126763626933098, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": -0.00844159908592701, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:2": -0.01015236