{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When meetings interrupt your hyperfocus, the real challenge is the transition—getting your brain back into the task after being pulled away. For someone with ADHD in a sedentary office job, here are the most effective, evidence-based transition routines:\n\n**1. Pre-Meeting \"Bookmark\":**\n- Before the meeting, jot down exactly where you are in your work and your next step. This acts as a mental bookmark, making it easier to re-engage after the meeting.\n\n**2. Quick Physical Reset:**\n- After the meeting, do a brief physical activity—stand up, stretch, or take a short walk. This helps reset your focus and signals your brain that it’s time to switch gears.\n\n**3. Structured Re-entry:**\n- Review your pre-meeting notes immediately after returning. Spend 1-2 minutes re-reading what you wrote and outlining your next micro-step. This minimizes the time lost to “ramping up” again.\n\n**4. Minimize New Distractions:**\n- Route calls and emails to voicemail or check them only at set times, especially right after meetings, so you don’t stack interruptions.\n\n**5. One Task at a Time:**\n- Resist the urge to start a new task post-meeting. Return to the one you were working on before the interruption.\n\nThese routines are tailored to your office environment and sedentary work style, helping you regain momentum quickly after meetings.\n\nWould you like a sample template for a pre/post-meeting checklist, or tips on communicating your need for uninterrupted time to colleagues?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:8": -0.021971849724650383, "clueweb22-en0017-60-15663:1": -0.02876484952867031, "clueweb22-en0030-54-15087:19": -0.037436794489622116, "clueweb22-en0038-88-03812:9": -0.038660235702991486, "clueweb22-en0025-75-16053:2": -0.045215290039777756}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I have a sedentary lifestyle.", "I live in G