{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-2_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "To get started with tattooing in Germany, especially since you already draw well and work in an office, here’s the most efficient, tailored path:\n\n**1. Build a Tattoo-Specific Portfolio**\n- Focus on designs that could translate well to tattoos (not just general art).\n- Show a range of styles and subject matter to demonstrate versatility.\n- This portfolio is your main tool for securing an apprenticeship.\n\n**2. Secure an Apprenticeship**\n- In Germany, as in most places, the standard route is an apprenticeship at a certified tattoo studio.\n- Apprenticeships are competitive; your strong drawing skills will help you stand out.\n- Expect to start with support tasks (cleaning, setup, customer service) before working on skin.\n- Apprenticeships typically last 1–3 years and are essential for learning hygiene, technique, and business practices.\n\n**3. Learn Hygiene and Certification Requirements**\n- Tattooing is highly regulated in Germany for health reasons. During your apprenticeship, you’ll learn about sterilization, safe practices, and local legal requirements.\n- Some regions may require you to complete specific health or safety courses.\n\n**4. Transition from Office to Studio**\n- Many start by working part-time or after hours, gradually shifting from office work as skills and opportunities grow.\n- Your sedentary lifestyle and office experience can be an asset for the administrative and customer service aspects of running a studio.\n\n**Next Step:** Would you like advice on building your portfolio, or tips on finding reputable studios offering apprenticeships in your area?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0010-92-01005:11": -0.027601365000009537, "clueweb22-en0028-52-08765:3": -0.03807423636317253, "clueweb22-en0028-52-08765:6": -0.04254555329680443, "clueweb22-en0025-87-09139:3": -0.045760683715343475, "clue