{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "8-2_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For self-taught tattooing as a hobbyist (focused on applying ink, not just design), the best online resources are structured courses that cover both technique and hygiene. Here’s what stands out:\n\n**Top Online Learning Options**\n- **Master Tattoo Class (Udemy):** Highly rated, beginner-friendly, and covers fundamentals like machine setup, needle types, line work, shading, and safety. Ideal for structured, step-by-step learning at your own pace.\n- **Beginners Guide to Tattooing:** Focuses on practical basics—machine handling, needle selection, and hygiene. Good for building foundational skills.\n- **How to Tattoo:** Another solid course for hands-on technique, including skin prep and aftercare.\n\n**Comprehensive Book + Online Portal**\n- **Basic Fundamentals of Modern Tattoo:** This book is paired with the “Teach Me To Tattoo” website, offering interactive Q&A and detailed tutorials on machine tuning, needle configurations, pigment selection, and sterilization. Especially useful if you prefer a mix of reading and online support.\n\n**Why These?**\n- They’re designed for beginners, require no prior studio experience, and emphasize safety—crucial for hobbyists practicing at home.\n- They include both video and written instruction, which suits different learning styles and helps with ADHD by breaking content into manageable chunks.\n\n**What to Avoid**\n- Random YouTube tutorials can be inconsistent and may skip critical hygiene steps.\n- General marketplaces for supplies or information, as quality and safety are not guaranteed.\n\nWould you like direct links to these courses, or tips on structuring your self-study practice sessions?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0046-30-10934:7": -0.05418582260608673, "clueweb22-en0031-07-10226:0": -0.08688343316316605, "clueweb22-en0031-07-10226:1": -0.15946301817893982, "cluewe