{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-1_2", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For mountain biking on forest trails and rough paths, saddle comfort is crucial. Here’s what matters most for your upgrade:\n\n**Saddle Types for Mountain Biking**\n- **Performance Saddles:** Designed for advanced riders, these are narrow and lightweight with minimal padding. Mountain bike versions add a bit more padding than road saddles to absorb bumps, but still prioritize thigh movement and prevent chafing. Ideal if you value agility and ride aggressively.\n- **Comfort Saddles:** Wider and more padded than performance saddles, these are found on touring or commuter bikes. They’re better for longer, less intense rides but can hinder pedaling efficiency on technical trails.\n- **MTB-Specific Saddles:** The best choice for you. These balance padding (especially for sit bones), durability, and an aerodynamic shape. They’re built to handle rough terrain and frequent position changes (standing, sitting back, tucking down).\n\n**Key Features to Look For**\n- **Padding:** Enough to cushion rough trails, but not so much that it causes chafing or restricts movement.\n- **Shape:** Slightly curved or flat, depending on your anatomy and riding style. A wavy shape can offer more support if you shift positions often.\n- **Durability:** Tough cover material to withstand mud, water, and abrasion.\n\n**Top Recommendation**\n- The Ergon SM Saddle (especially the women’s version, if relevant) is highly rated for mountain biking comfort and support.\n\nWould you like tips on saddle fitting, or help choosing between specific models?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-52-02129:7": -0.010454650036990643, "clueweb22-en0020-02-10977:1": -0.010994568467140198, "clueweb22-en0020-02-10977:0": -0.012163510546088219, "clueweb22-en0007-52-02129:8": -0.01301178801804781, "clueweb22-en0007-05-17998:10": -0.02011881396174431}, "ptkb_provenance":