{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "For upgrading your mountain bike pedals, here’s what matters most for your comfort, safety, and trail performance:\n\n**Top Priorities for New Pedals**\n- **Material Quality:** Aluminum or magnesium pedals are much more durable and impact-resistant than plastic. They’re ideal for rough trails and won’t crack or wear down quickly.\n- **Grip:** Look for flat platform pedals with metal traction pins. These pins keep your feet secure, especially in wet or muddy conditions. Adjustable or replaceable pins let you fine-tune grip to your preference.\n- **Pedal Shape:** A slightly concave platform helps your shoe “sit” into the pedal, improving stability and reducing foot fatigue. Larger platforms offer more support, but some riders prefer a slightly smaller size for technical maneuvering.\n- **Bearings:** Sealed bearings are smoother and last longer, especially if you ride in wet or dirty environments.\n- **Weight:** Lighter pedals (usually aluminum or magnesium) reduce fatigue on long rides, but don’t sacrifice durability for minimal weight.\n\n**What to Avoid**\n- Cheap plastic pedals—they wear out fast and can be slippery.\n- Pedals with overly sharp pins if you ride in shorts or value ease of foot repositioning.\n\n**Budget:** Quality flat pedals start around 30–50 euros. This is a worthwhile upgrade for comfort and control.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific pedal models, or tips on how to install them yourself?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0043-57-01365:4": -0.0057736532762646675, "clueweb22-en0043-57-01365:3": -0.006519595626741648, "clueweb22-en0002-05-13020:7": -0.0066389706917107105, "clueweb22-en0043-57-01365:5": -0.007916740141808987, "clueweb22-en0002-05-13020:4": -0.008807738311588764}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm a casual bike rider.", "I am not really handy with DIY.", "I value comfort over speed