{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_1", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Your bike chain keeps coming off likely due to one or more of these common issues:\n\n**Most Likely Causes (Given You’re Not Handy with DIY):**\n- **Chain Too Short or Too Loose:** If the chain was installed with too few links, or has stretched/worn out, it won’t stay on properly.\n- **Derailleur Misalignment:** The front or rear derailleur may be out of adjustment, causing the chain to shift off the gears.\n- **Worn or Incorrect Parts:** Worn sprocket teeth or the wrong size chain can also cause the chain to slip off.\n\n**Why This Matters for You:**\n- These are basic mechanical issues, but they require some mechanical skill and tools to diagnose and fix.\n- Attempting to adjust or replace parts without experience can make the problem worse.\n\n**What You Should Do:**\n- **Take your bike to a professional mechanic.** Explain that the chain keeps coming off, and mention the possible causes above. A mechanic can quickly check chain length, tension, derailleur alignment, and wear on parts.\n- This will save you time and frustration, and ensure your bike is safe to ride.\n\nWould you like tips on finding a reputable bike shop, or do you want to know what to expect during a repair visit?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0032-02-03041:7": -0.025050269439816475, "clueweb22-en0041-27-04552:13": -0.029477987438440323, "clueweb22-en0041-27-04552:0": -0.03670896962285042, "clueweb22-en0014-28-07476:1": -0.03962189704179764, "clueweb22-en0021-49-10678:12": -0.07354775071144104}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm a casual bike rider.", "I am not really handy with DIY."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0032-02-03041:7": -0.025050269439816475, "clueweb22-en0041-27-04552:13": -0.029477987438440323, "clueweb22-en0041-27-04552:0": -0.03670896962285042, "clueweb22-en0014-28-07476:1": -0.03962189704179764, "clueweb22-en0021-49-10678:12": -0.0735