{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_5", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Great question—here’s what you need to know:\n\n**What is the jockey on a bike?**\n- The \"jockey\" refers to the small wheels in your rear derailleur, also called jockey wheels or pulley wheels.\n- There are two: the upper (guide pulley) and the lower (tension pulley).\n    - The **guide pulley** (top) helps shift the chain between gears.\n    - The **tension pulley** (bottom) keeps the chain tight as you change gears.\n\n**Why does it matter for your chain issue?**\n- The jockey wheels guide and tension the chain. If they’re dirty, worn, or not spinning freely, your chain can jump off or run poorly—even if the derailleur is aligned.\n- A new chain on a new bike should work smoothly, so if the jockey wheels are sticky, damaged, or misaligned, that could be the culprit.\n\n**What should you do next?**\n- Spin both jockey wheels by hand. They should turn freely and quietly.\n- Check for excessive dirt, wear, or wobble.\n- If they’re dirty, clean and lube them. If worn or damaged, consider replacing them.\n\nWould you like a quick step-by-step on how to check and clean your jockey wheels, or do you want to move on to checking chain length and tension?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:2": -0.012918943539261818, "clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:1": -0.024738643318414688, "clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:0": -0.030389884486794472, "clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:3": -0.06520789861679077, "clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:0": -0.13491642475128174}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm a casual bike rider.", "I am not really handy with DIY.", "I like biking even in rainy weather and in the forest."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:2": -0.012918943539261818, "clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:1": -0.024738643318414688, "clueweb22-en0032-78-01787:0": -0.030389884486794472, "clueweb22-en0039-65-07416:3": -0.06520789861679077, "clueweb22-en0039-