{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_9", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "You asked if you can use car oil to lubricate your bike chain. Here’s the distilled, expert answer:\n\n**Best Option: Use Bike-Specific Chain Lubricant**\n- Bike chain lubes are designed to penetrate the chain, repel dirt and water, and protect against rust.\n- They minimize dirt buildup, which extends chain and gear life.\n- Look for mineral-based or wax-based lubes made for bicycles.\n\n**Why Not Car (Engine) Oil?**\n- Car oil is too thick for bike chains. It doesn’t penetrate well and can cause extra resistance, making pedaling harder and wearing out your drivetrain faster.\n- It attracts more dirt and grime, which accelerates wear.\n- It’s not formulated for the conditions a bike chain faces.\n\n**Other Household Oils?**\n- Vegetable or cooking oils can work as a very short-term emergency fix, but they attract dirt, go rancid, and don’t protect against rust or moisture.\n- Spray lubricants like WD-40 are too thin and not suitable as a chain lube.\n\n**What To Do Now**\n- Clean your chain if needed.\n- Apply a bike-specific chain lubricant (wet lube for wet conditions, dry lube for dry).\n- Wipe off excess to avoid attracting dirt.\n\nWould you like recommendations for specific bike chain lubes, or tips on how to apply them for best results?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0019-98-16645:10": -0.010541589930653572, "clueweb22-en0019-98-16645:13": -0.011595236137509346, "clueweb22-en0024-04-10077:3": -0.016260970383882523, "clueweb22-en0003-70-07570:4": -0.016792606562376022, "clueweb22-en0024-04-10077:2": -0.018059348687529564}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I sometimes get overwhelmed by too many technical choices.", "I'm a casual bike rider.", "I am not really handy with DIY.", "I like biking even in rainy weather and in the forest."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0019-98-16645:10": -0.010541589930653572, "clueweb22-en00