{"metadata": {"team_id": "grilllab", "run_id": "grilllab-larf-finetuned", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Your best indicator for when to re-lubricate your bike chain is sound and feel—not a set schedule.\n\n**How to Know When to Lube:**\n- **Listen:** If your chain starts making noise (squeaking, grinding, or sounding dry), it needs lube. A quiet, smooth chain is well-lubricated.\n- **Look:** If the chain looks dry, rusty, or you see visible dirt/grime, it’s time to clean and re-lube.\n- **Feel:** If pedaling feels rough or shifting isn’t smooth, that’s another sign.\n\n**Best Practices:**\n- **Wipe Down After Every Ride:** Quickly wipe your chain with a rag after each ride to remove dust and old lube. This prolongs chain life and keeps it running smoothly.\n- **Clean Before Lubing:** Always clean the chain before applying new lube—otherwise, dirt will get trapped and accelerate wear.\n- **Type of Lube:** Use a bicycle-specific chain lube suited to your riding conditions (wet, dry, or all-purpose). Avoid car oil—it attracts dirt and isn’t designed for bike chains.\n\n**Typical Frequency:**\n- **After Wet or Muddy Rides:** Clean and lube immediately.\n- **Dry Conditions:** Lube every 100–200 miles, or when you notice noise.\n- **Heavy Use:** More frequent checks and lubing may be needed.\n\nWould you like a quick guide on how to clean and lube your chain for best results?", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0002-63-04266:1": -0.005363005213439465, "clueweb22-en0002-63-04266:2": -0.006426264997571707, "clueweb22-en0005-80-03758:10": -0.010234376415610313, "clueweb22-en0019-16-02034:6": -0.0115036740899086, "clueweb22-en0028-70-13001:4": -0.015034692361950874}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I'm a casual bike rider.", "I am not really handy with DIY.", "I like biking even in rainy weather and in the forest."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0002-63-04266:1": -0.005363005213439465, "clueweb22-en0002-63-04266:2": -0.006426264997571707, "c